!type: Basic Math
!deck: Math::Calculus::Integration
!tags: Math Calculus Integration Theory

!id: zGlwKahZ6l
!front: When substituting \( y=f(x) \) in \( \int_{f(a)}^{f(b)} f^{-1}(y)\,dy \), how do the bounds change?
!back:
Assume \( f \) is one-to-one on the chosen interval. Since \( x=f^{-1}(y) \), the lower bound \( y=f(a) \) becomes \( x=a \), and the upper bound \( y=f(b) \) becomes \( x=b \). Thus
\[
    \int_{f(a)}^{f(b)} f^{-1}(y)\,dy
    = \int_a^b f^{-1}(f(x))f'(x)\,dx.
\]
The ordered bounds \( a \) to \( b \) are still correct if \( f \) is decreasing; the reversed order \( f(a)>f(b) \) is already part of the directed integral in \( y \).
~~~

!id: hApfgMtDMJ
!front: Derive the definite integral identity for \( \int_{f(a)}^{f(b)} f^{-1}(y)\,dy \).
!back:
Assume \( f \) is one-to-one and continuously differentiable on \( [a,b] \). Substitute \( y = f(x) \), so \( dy = f'(x)\,dx \). As \( y \) moves from \( f(a) \) to \( f(b) \), \( x \) moves from \( a \) to \( b \), hence
\[
\begin{align*}
    \int_{f(a)}^{f(b)} f^{-1}(y)\,dy
        &= \int_a^b f^{-1}(\underbrace{f(x)}_{y}) \underbrace{f'(x) \, dx}_{dy} \\
        &= \int_a^b x f'(x)\,dx \quad \text{(integrate by parts)} \\
        &= \left[x f(x)\right]_a^b - \int_a^b f(x)\,dx \\
        &= b f(b) - a f(a) - \int_a^b f(x)\,dx.
\end{align*}
\]
!extra: The bounds are oriented: if \( f \) is decreasing, then \( f(a) > f(b) \), and the identity still uses the directed integral from \( f(a) \) to \( f(b) \).
~~~

!id: InpkHt4xmD
!front: Use the inverse-function integral identity to derive \( \int \arcsin y\,dy \).
!back:
Restrict \( \sin x \) to \( [-\pi/2,\pi/2] \), so its inverse is \( \arcsin y \). The indefinite inverse-function identity gives
\[
\begin{align*}
    \int \arcsin y\,dy
        &= y\arcsin y - \int \sin x\,dx \\
        &= y\arcsin y + \cos x + C \\
        &= y\arcsin y + \sqrt{1-y^2} + C.
\end{align*}
\]
!extra: Here \( x=\arcsin y \), and \( \cos x=\sqrt{1-y^2} \) on \( [-\pi/2,\pi/2] \).
~~~

!id: Izxx9gxdEc
!front: Use the inverse-function integral identity to derive \( \int \arccos y\,dy \).
!back:
Restrict \( \cos x \) to \( [0,\pi] \), so its inverse is \( \arccos y \). The indefinite inverse-function identity gives
\[
\begin{align*}
    \int \arccos y\,dy
        &= y\arccos y - \int \cos x\,dx \\
        &= y\arccos y - \sin x + C \\
        &= y\arccos y - \sqrt{1-y^2} + C.
\end{align*}
\]
!extra: Here \( x=\arccos y \), and \( \sin x=\sqrt{1-y^2} \) on \( [0,\pi] \).
~~~

!id: whcJkPMldo
!front: Use the inverse-function integral identity to derive \( \int \arctan y\,dy \).
!back:
Restrict \( \tan x \) to \( (-\pi/2,\pi/2) \), so its inverse is \( \arctan y \). The indefinite inverse-function identity gives
\[
\begin{align*}
    \int \arctan y\,dy
        &= y\arctan y - \int \tan x\,dx \\
        &= y\arctan y + \ln(\cos x) + C \\
        &= y\arctan y - \frac{1}{2}\ln(1+y^2) + C.
\end{align*}
\]
!extra: Here \( x=\arctan y \), and \( \cos x=1/\sqrt{1+y^2} \) on \( (-\pi/2,\pi/2) \).
~~~

!id: 9mQE1PmQ8m
!front: Use the inverse-function integral identity to derive \( \int \ln y\,dy \).
!back:
Since \( \ln y \) is the inverse of \( e^x \), the indefinite inverse-function identity gives
\[
\begin{align*}
    \int \ln y\,dy
        &= y\ln y - \int e^x\,dx \\
        &= y\ln y - e^x + C \\
        &= y\ln y - y + C.
\end{align*}
\]
!extra: Here \( x=\ln y \), so \( e^x=y \). This is for \( y>0 \).
~~~
